+++
title = "Calendar Feeds"
template = "docs"
+++

The Source.Calendar plugin **downloads iCal feeds** (`.ics` URLs) into your `source/` folder. Use it together with the [Availability Calendar](../calendar/) plugin: your booking platform or calendar app keeps the bookings, and Revela fetches the latest version before each build.

<div class="features-grid">

### 🔗 Any iCal URL

Works with the export or subscription link of Google Calendar, Outlook, Airbnb, Booking.com and most booking tools.

### 🗂️ Named Feeds

Configure several feeds and fetch all of them, or just one by name.

### 🛡️ Safe Updates

If a download fails, the previous file is kept, so your calendar never ends up empty.

</div>

## Installation

The **Standalone** edition has Source.Calendar built in. In the **Full** edition, install it from the bundled packages:

```bash
revela plugin install Source.Calendar
```

## Configuration

Add your feeds to `project.json` below `plugins.calendarFeeds`. Each feed has a name, the URL to download, and the file to write, relative to your `source` folder:

```json
{
  "plugins": {
    "calendarFeeds": {
      "feeds": {
        "bookings": {
          "url": "https://calendar.example.com/export/bookings.ics",
          "output": "availability/bookings.ics"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

| Option | Required | Description |
|--------|----------|-------------|
| `url` | Yes | The iCal feed URL (`http` or `https`) |
| `output` | Yes | Target file below the source folder, for example `availability/bookings.ics` |

- The `output` file must lie inside your source folder, and every feed needs its own file.
- If your calendar app gives you a `webcal://` link, replace `webcal://` with `https://`.
- Links to your own computer or local network (such as `localhost` or `192.168.x.x`) are refused.

There is no `revela config` command for feeds; edit `project.json` directly.

Feed URLs often contain a private token. To keep it out of `project.json` (for example in CI), set it as an environment variable instead:

```bash
SPECTARA__REVELA__PLUGINS__CALENDARFEEDS__FEEDS__BOOKINGS__URL=https://calendar.example.com/export/bookings.ics?token=...
SPECTARA__REVELA__PLUGINS__CALENDARFEEDS__FEEDS__BOOKINGS__OUTPUT=availability/bookings.ics
```

## Usage

```bash
# Download all configured feeds
revela source calendar fetch

# Download only one feed
revela source calendar fetch --name bookings
```

```
✓ bookings → availability/bookings.ics

Fetched 1 feed(s) (4.2 KB)
```

Fetching is **not** part of `revela generate all`. Run it before you build:

```bash
revela source calendar fetch
revela generate all
```

Then point a calendar page at the downloaded file with `calendar.source = "bookings.ics"` in `source/availability/_index.revela` (see [Availability Calendar](../calendar/)).

In a CI workflow, add `./revela source calendar fetch` before `./revela generate all` (see [Deployment](../../guide/deployment/)), and run the workflow on a schedule so the calendar stays up to date.

## Error Handling

- Each download times out after 30 seconds.
- A feed larger than 10 MB is rejected; the previous file stays untouched.
- A failed download leaves the previous file untouched and prints which feed failed. The other feeds are still fetched.
- The command exits with `1` if any feed failed, so scripts can react.
- Without any configured feeds, the command shows an example configuration.

If the calendar page complains about a missing or broken file afterwards, run `revela check calendar`.

## CLI Commands

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `revela source calendar fetch` | Download all configured feeds |
| `revela source calendar fetch --name <feed>` | Download one feed (`-n`) |
